# openHC — BCM53125 managed switch (DSA/b53) kernel side. Shared by every EA
# variant that HAS the switch: ea3-v1, ea3-v2, ea1-v2-poe.
#
# Three pieces are needed and all three are here:
#   1. the SPI master     — spi-pxa2xx-pci binds PCI 8086:2e6a (the CE4100 port,
#                           which the CE5310 also exposes)
#   2. the switch driver  — b53 with its SPI binding
#   3. board description  — ea-common/drivers/spi/spi-ea-b53-board.c
#                           registers the switch at CS 1 on the SPI bus whose
#                           number is the controller's PCI devfn (92), and hands DSA
#                           the measured port map (CPU=5, jacks=1 and 2) as
#                           platform_data, since there is no DTB.
#
# Piece 3 previously did not build (it included <net/dsa.h> for dsa_chip_data,
# which moved to <linux/platform_data/dsa.h>); that is fixed. What is still
# UNVERIFIED is DSA's bring-up of port 1, which the stock switch configuration
# isolates — DSA has to reconfigure the switch, not merely describe it. If DSA
# does not attach, S40net falls back to DHCP on the master and only the PRIMARY
# jack works (measured), so the board stays reachable either way.
#
# COMMON_CLK is not optional here even though nothing on this board has a clock
# tree worth speaking of. SPI_PXA2XX_PCI is `def_tristate SPI_PXA2XX && PCI &&
# COMMON_CLK` — a derived symbol, so setting CONFIG_SPI_PXA2XX_PCI directly in a
# fragment is silently DROPPED by kconfig. Without COMMON_CLK the glue is simply
# never compiled: the SPI master never binds 8086:2e6a, bus 0 never registers,
# the board_info our patch queues never instantiates, and b53 never probes.
# Measured exactly that on hardware: 01:0b.4 with no driver bound and an empty
# /sys/class/spi_master. Do not "clean up" the line below.
CONFIG_COMMON_CLK=y
CONFIG_SPI=y
CONFIG_SPI_MASTER=y
CONFIG_SPI_PXA2XX=y
#
# The b53 symbols are CONFIG_B53 / CONFIG_B53_SPI_DRIVER — NOT
# CONFIG_NET_DSA_B53*, which is what this fragment said for a long time. Those
# names do not exist, so kconfig dropped them silently and b53 was simply never
# built: on hardware the SPI device spi92.1 appeared with modalias
# spi:bcm53125 and /sys/bus/spi/drivers/ was empty, because there was no driver
# to bind it. (b53's own Kconfig: `menuconfig B53` / `config B53_SPI_DRIVER`.)
# B53 selects NET_DSA_TAG_BRCM and NET_DSA_TAG_NONE itself.
CONFIG_NET_DSA=y
CONFIG_B53=y
CONFIG_B53_SPI_DRIVER=y
CONFIG_NET_DSA_TAG_BRCM=y
CONFIG_NET_DSA_TAG_BRCM_PREPEND=y
CONFIG_BRIDGE=y
CONFIG_VLAN_8021Q=y
